 ## Overview
-Extended Phase Range SLM Modulation is a technique used in simultaneous color holography to mitigate depth replicas (ghost images) by exploiting the wavelength-dependent phase response of Liquid Crystal Spatial Light Modulators (SLSMs) [S1]. Unlike standard 0-2$\pi$ SLMs which produce perfect intensity copies of the target image at specific replica planes, this method uses SLMs with extended phase ranges (greater than 2$\pi$) that vary significantly across RGB wavelengths to break the wavelength-depth ambiguity [S1]. This approach sets it apart from [Computational Phase Modulation (CGH Generation)](7284-computational-phase-modulation-cgh-generation.md) by specifically addressing the multi-wavelength crosstalk inherent in single-SLM color holography, and differs from [Phase-Only Hologram Approximations](13999-phase-only-hologram-approximations.md) by relying on physical device dispersion rather than algorithmic approximation to suppress artifacts.
+Extended Phase Range SLM Modulation is a technique used in simultaneous color holography to mitigate depth replicas (ghost images) by exploiting the wavelength-dependent phase response of Liquid Crystal Spatial Light Modulators (SLMs) [S1]. Unlike standard 0-2$\pi$ SLMs which produce perfect intensity copies of the target image at specific replica planes, this method uses SLMs with extended phase ranges (greater than 2$\pi$) that vary significantly across RGB wavelengths to break the wavelength-depth ambiguity [S1]. This approach sets it apart from [Computational Phase Modulation (CGH Generation)](7284-computational-phase-modulation-cgh-generation.md) by specifically addressing the multi-wavelength crosstalk inherent in single-SLM color holography, and differs from [Phase-Only Hologram Approximations](13999-phase-only-hologram-approximations.md) by relying on physical device dispersion rather than algorithmic approximation to suppress artifacts [G].
 
